/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · life everyday (Australia) */
WB.L("medicare-and-finding-a-gp", "life", "beginner", "Medicare and finding a GP you trust", "Most Australians carry a green card in their wallet without really knowing what it does. Here is how Medicare works in practice, and how to find a doctor who will know you by name.",
  [
    ["What Medicare actually covers", "Medicare pays a set rebate for many medical services and free treatment as a public patient in public hospitals.", "Picture two people seeing the same GP on the same morning. One walks out paying nothing, the other pays at the desk and gets part of it back a day later. Both are using Medicare. Medicare is Australia's public health scheme, and it does two main things. It pays a rebate for a long list of out of hospital services, such as GP visits, many specialist consultations, blood tests and scans, based on a fee set by the government. It also covers treatment as a public patient in a public hospital at no cost. It does not cover most dental care, glasses, ambulance trips in most states or private hospital accommodation. This is general information, and the details are on servicesaustralia.gov.au."],
    ["Getting your card and the app", "Enrol through Services Australia, then use the Express Plus Medicare app or your myGov account to manage it.", "Citizens and permanent residents can enrol in Medicare, as can some visitors from countries with reciprocal agreements. Most Australians are enrolled as babies on a parent's card, and you can apply for your own card from age 15. Once enrolled, you can link Medicare to your myGov account, which lets you see your claims history, update bank details so rebates land in your account, and download a digital copy of your card. The Express Plus Medicare app does much the same on your phone. Keep your bank details current: if the clinic charges you upfront, the rebate is often paid straight back to you, sometimes within minutes. Check who is on your card too, so a teenager heading off to university has their own."],
    ["Bulk billing versus paying a gap", "Bulk billing means the doctor accepts the Medicare rebate as full payment; otherwise you pay the difference.", "When a GP bulk bills, they claim the rebate directly from Medicare and you pay nothing. When they do not, they charge their own fee, and you pay the gap between that fee and the rebate. Gaps vary widely between clinics and even between doctors in the same clinic, so it is worth asking at the desk before you book, not after. Many practices bulk bill some groups, such as children or concession card holders, while charging others. The government has expanded incentives for bulk billing in recent years, so the picture is changing. The quickest way to check is simply to ring and ask: 'Do you bulk bill, and if not, what will I be out of pocket for a standard consult?'"],
    ["The safety net for heavy years", "If your out of pocket costs pile up in a calendar year, the Medicare Safety Net can raise your rebates.", "Some years are expensive. A run of specialist visits, scans and tests can add up quickly, especially for out of hospital care. The Medicare Safety Net is designed for this. Once your gap payments for eligible services pass a threshold in a calendar year, Medicare pays a higher rebate for the rest of that year. Individuals are registered automatically, but couples and families need to register together so their costs are combined, and many people forget. You can do this through your Medicare online account in myGov. The thresholds change each year, so check the current figures on the Services Australia website rather than relying on an old number you heard from a friend."],
    ["Why one regular GP matters", "Seeing the same GP over time means better care, because they know your history and notice changes.", "A GP who has seen you for years remembers that your blood pressure crept up last winter, that your father had heart trouble and that you hate taking tablets. That memory is valuable. Continuity of care is linked with better management of long term conditions and fewer unnecessary tests. Australia now has MyMedicare, a voluntary scheme where you formally register with one practice. Registration can unlock longer telehealth consultations with that practice and helps the clinic plan your care. Even without registering, try to stick with one clinic and, where possible, one doctor. Ask for a longer appointment when you have several issues, rather than squeezing everything into a standard visit and leaving the important question until the door handle."],
    ["How to find a good one", "Look for a GP who listens, explains clearly and works with you, and do not be afraid to try another.", "Start with healthdirect's Service Finder at healthdirect.gov.au, which lists practices near you along with their opening hours and whether they bulk bill. Friends and neighbours are another good source: ask what they like about their doctor, not just whether the clinic is convenient. On your first visit, notice whether the GP listens without rushing, explains things in plain language and asks what matters to you. Some people prefer a doctor of a particular gender, or one who speaks their language, and clinics can usually tell you this. If the fit is not right after a visit or two, it is perfectly fine to move on. You can ask the old practice to transfer your records to the new one."],
    ["After hours and when to go elsewhere", "Know your options outside clinic hours, and call triple zero in an emergency.", "Illness rarely keeps business hours. For advice at any time, the healthdirect helpline on 1800 022 222 connects you with a registered nurse who can help you decide whether to see a GP, go to an urgent care clinic or head to hospital. Many areas now have Medicare Urgent Care Clinics, which treat urgent but not life threatening problems, such as minor fractures or cuts needing stitches, often without an appointment and without a fee. Telehealth GP services can handle many simple issues by phone or video. For chest pain, trouble breathing, signs of stroke or serious injury, call triple zero (000) straight away. Saving these options in your phone now means you will not be searching in a panic later."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which of these does Medicare generally NOT cover?", ["GP visits", "Public patient treatment in a public hospital", "Most dental care", "Many blood tests"], 2, "Most dental care, glasses and private hospital accommodation sit outside Medicare."],
    [2, "What does bulk billing mean?", ["The doctor charges a discounted private fee", "The doctor accepts the Medicare rebate as full payment", "You pay upfront and claim later", "Your health fund pays the bill"], 1, "With bulk billing the doctor claims the rebate directly and you pay nothing."],
    [3, "Why should couples and families register for the Medicare Safety Net?", ["So their costs are combined toward the threshold", "So they get a free GP", "It is required for a Medicare card", "So they can skip waiting lists"], 0, "Individuals are registered automatically, but families must register so their costs count together."],
    [4, "What is MyMedicare?", ["A private health fund", "A voluntary scheme to register with one regular practice", "A new kind of Medicare card", "A hospital booking system"], 1, "MyMedicare lets you formally register with one practice to support continuity of care."],
    [6, "Which service lets you speak to a registered nurse at any hour?", ["Smartraveller", "The ACCC hotline", "The myGov help desk", "The healthdirect helpline"], 3, "The healthdirect helpline, 1800 022 222, connects you with a registered nurse."]
  ]);

WB.L("mygov-and-government-services", "life", "beginner", "myGov without the headache", "One login for Centrelink, Medicare, the tax office and more sounds simple, until you are stuck on hold. Here is how to make the system work for you.",
  [
    ["One front door", "myGov is a single secure account that links many federal government services.", "Imagine you have just moved house. In the old days you might have filled in separate forms for the tax office, Medicare and Centrelink. With myGov, you sign in once at my.gov.au and update your details for several services together. myGov is not a service itself, more like a front door with a set of keys. Behind it sit linked services such as the Australian Taxation Office, Medicare, Centrelink, Child Support, My Health Record and the Department of Veterans' Affairs, along with some state services. You choose which ones to link. Each service still holds its own records, so linking simply lets you reach them all from one place and receive their letters in one inbox."],
    ["Setting up an account properly", "Create your account with a personal email, strong sign-in and your own details, never someone else's.", "Go directly to my.gov.au by typing the address yourself, and create an account using an email address only you can access. Avoid a shared family email or a work address you might lose when you change jobs. You will then choose how to sign in. Options have included a code sent by text, an authenticator app, passkeys and the myID app, which is the government's Digital ID. Stronger options make it much harder for someone else to break in. Each adult in a household should have their own account, even couples who share everything else. Some services require you to prove your identity before linking, so have documents such as your driver licence, passport or Medicare card handy."],
    ["Linking services", "To link a service you answer questions only you should know, using details from your records.", "Linking is where many people get stuck. When you link the ATO, for instance, you may be asked for details from a past tax return or payment summary. Linking Medicare asks for your card number and recent claim details. Centrelink can generate a linking code if you call or visit, which is handy when you cannot answer the questions. These checks exist to stop impostors, so treat them as protection rather than a nuisance. Before you start, gather a recent payslip, your tax file number, your Medicare card and any letters from the agency. Doing it in one sitting at a desk, rather than on a phone in a car park, saves a lot of frustration."],
    ["Reading your inbox", "Government letters now arrive in your myGov inbox, so turn on notifications and check them.", "Once services are linked, many will stop posting paper letters and send them to your myGov inbox instead. That includes notices about payments, debts, tax assessments and reviews, some with deadlines. Missing one can be costly, for example if Centrelink asks you to confirm your income and your payment is paused when you do not reply. Turn on email or text notifications so you know when something arrives, but remember that the notification will never contain a link to sign in. Make a habit of opening the inbox whenever you are alerted, reading the letter in full and saving important ones as PDFs. A simple folder on your computer for government letters makes tax time and disputes far easier."],
    ["Spotting myGov scams", "Scammers copy myGov branding, so never sign in through a link in a text or email.", "myGov is a favourite disguise for scammers. A text might say you are owed a refund or that your account has been suspended, with a link to a convincing fake site that steals your login. Services Australia has said that myGov texts and emails will not contain links asking you to sign in. The safe habit is simple: whatever the message says, close it, open your browser and type my.gov.au yourself, or use the official app. If you have entered your details on a fake page, change your password straight away, and contact Services Australia for help securing your account. You can also report scam messages to Scamwatch, run by the ACCC, at scamwatch.gov.au."],
    ["Getting through to a person", "Use online options first, and prepare before you call so one call does the job.", "Most routine tasks, such as updating an address, reporting income or claiming a Medicare rebate, can be done online or in the app, often faster than waiting on the phone. When you do need a person, prepare before you dial. Have your customer reference number, your myGov sign-in, and a short note of what you need and what has happened so far. Calls are usually quieter early in the morning and later in the week, though this varies. Write down the date, the name or identifier of the person you spoke to and what they said. Service centres can help in person, and Services Australia also offers interpreter services and support for people who are not comfortable online."],
    ["When things go wrong", "If you disagree with a decision, ask for an explanation, then a formal review, and keep records.", "Government decisions are not always right. If Centrelink cuts a payment or raises a debt you think is wrong, you have options. First, ask for an explanation of how the decision was made. You can then request a formal review, where someone not involved in the original decision looks again. Time limits can apply, especially if you want any back pay, so act quickly. Beyond that, independent bodies such as the Administrative Review Tribunal can review many decisions, and the Commonwealth Ombudsman investigates complaints about how agencies have treated you. Free legal help is available through community legal centres and Economic Justice Australia's network. This is general information; check the details for your situation on servicesaustralia.gov.au."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is myGov best described as?", ["A government payment", "A single front door linking many government services", "A health insurance scheme", "A tax agent"], 1, "myGov is one secure account that links services like the ATO, Medicare and Centrelink."],
    [1, "Which email is best for a myGov account?", ["A shared family address", "A work email", "A personal address only you can access", "Any address, it does not matter"], 2, "A personal email you control avoids losing access or sharing it with others."],
    [3, "Why should you turn on myGov inbox notifications?", ["So you never miss letters that may have deadlines", "To get discounts", "To receive sign-in links", "Because paper letters are illegal now"], 0, "Many agencies send important letters only to the inbox, some with deadlines."],
    [4, "A text says your myGov account is suspended and gives a link. What should you do?", ["Click the link quickly", "Reply with your password", "Forward it to friends", "Ignore the link and type my.gov.au yourself"], 3, "Official myGov messages will not include sign-in links, so go to the site directly."],
    [6, "If you disagree with a Centrelink decision, what is usually the first formal step after asking for an explanation?", ["Request a formal review", "Go straight to the High Court", "Write to your MP only", "Stop all payments"], 0, "A formal review by someone not involved in the original decision is the usual next step."]
  ]);

WB.L("buying-a-used-car", "life", "intermediate", "Buying a used car without getting burned", "A shiny paint job can hide a lot. With a few checks and a cool head, you can buy a second hand car that is exactly what it seems.",
  [
    ["Decide before you browse", "Set a total budget and a shortlist of models before you look at a single listing.", "Scrolling car listings late at night is a fast way to fall in love with the wrong vehicle. Start instead with a plan. Work out what you need the car to do: daily commuting, towing, long country drives or squeezing into tight city parks. Then set a total budget that includes transfer costs, stamp duty, insurance and a buffer for early repairs, not just the sticker price. Research a shortlist of reliable models in that range using independent reviews and safety ratings from ANCAP, the Australasian New Car Assessment Program. Also check running costs such as fuel use, servicing and insurance premiums. When you know which three or four models suit you, every listing becomes easier to judge calmly."],
    ["Dealer, private or auction", "Each way of buying carries different protections, prices and risks.", "Licensed dealers usually charge more, but you get extra protection. In several states, dealers must provide a statutory warranty on cars under a certain age and kilometres, and some states give a short cooling-off period on dealer sales. Consumer guarantees under Australian Consumer Law also apply. Private sales are often cheaper, but you have far fewer rights: the car is largely sold as it is, and there is generally no cooling-off period. Auctions can offer bargains, yet you usually cannot test drive and your recourse is limited. The rules differ by state, so check your state's fair trading or consumer affairs website before you buy. This is general information, not legal advice."],
    ["Run a PPSR check", "A PPSR search shows whether a car has money owing, has been written off or is reported stolen.", "Here is a nasty surprise some buyers get: months after buying a car privately, a finance company turns up to repossess it because the seller still owed money on a loan secured against it. The Personal Property Securities Register exists to prevent this. For a small fee, a search at ppsr.gov.au using the car's VIN (vehicle identification number) tells you whether there is security interest registered over the car, and whether it has been recorded as written off or stolen. Do the search on the day you pay, because a seller could take out a loan between your first look and the sale. Save the certificate. Make sure the VIN on the paperwork matches the one stamped on the car itself."],
    ["Inspect it like a sceptic", "View the car in daylight, start it cold and look for signs that do not match the story.", "Arrange to see the car in daylight, at the seller's home if it is a private sale, so you know where they live. Ask the seller not to start the engine before you arrive, because a cold start can reveal rattles, smoke and hard starting that disappear once it is warm. Look for uneven panel gaps or paint that does not quite match, which can point to accident repairs. Check tyre wear, under the oil cap for milky residue and under the car for leaks. Ask for the service book and receipts, and make sure the kilometres and dates make sense. Compare the name on the registration papers with the seller's licence. If anything feels rushed or evasive, walk away."],
    ["Test drive with purpose", "Drive on a mix of roads and listen, feel and test everything, from brakes to air conditioning.", "A good test drive lasts at least twenty minutes and covers suburban streets, a stretch of highway and, ideally, a hill. Turn the radio off. Listen for knocks over bumps, whining from the gearbox and squealing brakes. Notice whether the car pulls to one side when you brake hard on a clear, safe road or let go of the wheel briefly on a straight. Automatic gear changes should be smooth, and a manual clutch should not slip. Test every switch: windows, lights, wipers, air conditioning, heating, reversing camera and locks. Check that warning lights come on briefly at start-up and then go out, since a light that never appears may have been disconnected."],
    ["Get a professional opinion", "A pre-purchase inspection by an independent mechanic is cheap insurance on an expensive purchase.", "Even confident car people miss things. An independent pre-purchase inspection, from a mechanic you choose or a motoring club such as the NRMA, RACV or RACQ, typically costs a few hundred dollars and can uncover rust, hidden accident damage, worn suspension or engine problems. That report is also a powerful negotiating tool: if it finds worn brake pads and an overdue timing belt, you can reasonably ask for the cost to come off the price. Never let the seller choose the mechanic. If a seller refuses to allow an inspection, treat that as a red flag. Remember to also check registration status and what safety or roadworthy certificate your state requires before transfer."],
    ["Paying and transferring safely", "Pay securely, get a written receipt and lodge the transfer with your state's transport agency promptly.", "Online car scams often follow a pattern: a great price, a seller who is overseas or in the army, and a request for a deposit before you can see the car. Never pay for a car you have not inspected in person. When you are ready to buy, pay by bank transfer or bank cheque rather than carrying large amounts of cash, and get a written receipt with both names, the date, the price, the VIN and the registration number. Both buyer and seller usually have to complete a transfer form, and you will pay stamp duty and a transfer fee within a set number of days. Arrange insurance before you drive away, not the next morning."]
  ],
  [
    [2, "What can a PPSR search reveal about a used car?", ["Its fuel economy", "Whether money is owing, or it is written off or stolen", "Its resale value next year", "The previous owner's driving record"], 1, "The PPSR shows registered security interests and written off or stolen status."],
    [1, "Compared with buying from a dealer, a private sale usually gives you", ["More legal protection", "A mandatory statutory warranty", "Fewer rights, as the car is largely sold as is", "An automatic cooling-off period"], 2, "Private sales are often cheaper but carry far fewer protections."],
    [3, "Why ask the seller not to start the car before you arrive?", ["To save fuel", "A cold start can reveal problems that disappear once warm", "It is a legal requirement", "So the battery stays charged"], 1, "Rattles, smoke and hard starting are easier to spot from cold."],
    [5, "Who should choose the mechanic for a pre-purchase inspection?", ["You, the buyer", "The seller", "The finance company", "The previous owner's mechanic"], 0, "An inspector you choose is independent of the seller."],
    [6, "A seller says they are overseas and asks for a deposit before you can see the car. This is", ["Normal practice", "A common scam pattern", "Required by law", "A sign of a bargain"], 1, "Never pay for a car you have not inspected in person."]
  ]);

WB.L("travelling-overseas-smoothly", "life", "beginner", "Travelling overseas without the drama", "The best trips start long before the airport. A little preparation with your passport, the right insurance and official travel advice can save you a world of trouble.",
  [
    ["Check your passport first", "Many countries want your passport valid for months beyond your trip, so check the expiry date early.", "Every year some travellers arrive at the check-in desk only to be turned away because their passport expires too soon. Many countries require your passport to be valid for at least six months beyond the date you arrive or leave, and some also need blank pages for stamps. So the first job, ideally months before you fly, is to find your passport and look at the expiry date. Australian passports are issued by the Australian Passport Office, and you can apply or renew through passports.gov.au. Processing times vary and can stretch during busy seasons, so leave plenty of time. Paying for priority processing is possible but costs extra and is not always available."],
    ["Visas and entry rules", "Check entry rules for every country you visit, including those you only pass through.", "Australian passport holders can visit many countries without a visa, but not all, and the rules change. Some places require an electronic travel authorisation applied for online before departure, such as the United States ESTA or the United Kingdom's ETA. Others need a full visa from an embassy. Do not forget countries where you only change planes, as some transit stops have their own rules. The safest source is the official government website of the country you are visiting, linked from its Smartraveller page. Be wary of third party websites that look official but charge large fees to submit a form you could lodge yourself. Also check rules about medicines you plan to carry, since some common ones are restricted overseas."],
    ["Read Smartraveller", "Smartraveller is the Australian government's travel advice service, with a level for every destination.", "Smartraveller, at smartraveller.gov.au, is run by the Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade. Each destination has a travel advice page with an overall advice level, from 'Exercise normal safety precautions' through 'Exercise a high degree of caution' and 'Reconsider your need to travel' to 'Do not travel'. Different regions within a country can carry different levels. The pages also cover local laws, health risks, scams, natural hazards and entry requirements. Read the advice for every country before you book and again before you leave, since it can change quickly. You can subscribe to email updates for your destinations. Insurers may refuse to cover you in places marked 'Do not travel', so the level matters for your policy too."],
    ["Buy travel insurance that fits", "Get insurance when you book, and read what it excludes, especially pre-existing conditions and activities.", "Smartraveller puts it bluntly: if you cannot afford travel insurance, you cannot afford to travel. Medical treatment overseas can cost tens of thousands of dollars, and an evacuation flight home far more. Buy cover as soon as you book, so cancellation cover protects you if something goes wrong before you leave. Then read the product disclosure statement, not just the brochure. Check how pre-existing medical conditions are handled, the excess you pay per claim, and exclusions for things such as riding a scooter without the right licence, extreme sports, or incidents involving alcohol. Credit card insurance can be useful but often has strict conditions. This is general information, so compare policies carefully for your situation."],
    ["Money, phones and documents", "Tell your bank you are travelling, sort out phone roaming and keep copies of key documents.", "A card blocked for suspicious overseas activity can leave you stranded at a hotel desk. Check whether your bank wants to know your travel plans, and carry at least two ways to pay, kept in different places. Compare fees: some cards charge international transaction fees on every purchase, while travel cards and some bank accounts do not. Before you leave, check your phone plan's roaming charges, which can be huge, or plan to buy a local SIM or eSIM. Save digital copies of your passport, insurance policy and bookings somewhere you can reach from any device, and share them with someone at home. A printed copy of your insurer's emergency number in your wallet is a smart backup."],
    ["Your health on the road", "See a GP or travel clinic well before departure for vaccinations and medicine advice.", "Some vaccinations need several weeks to take effect, or more than one dose, so book a GP or travel clinic six to eight weeks before departure if you can. Bring a list of medicines and a letter from your doctor describing them, and keep them in original labelled packaging. Australia has reciprocal health care agreements with some countries, including the United Kingdom, New Zealand and Ireland, which can cover medically necessary treatment, but they are limited and do not replace insurance. On long flights, move around and drink water. Healthdirect and Smartraveller both have practical pages on travel health. This is general information, not medical advice, so talk to a health professional about your own needs."],
    ["When things go wrong", "Know what the Australian government can and cannot do for you overseas.", "If your passport is stolen, report it to local police and contact the nearest Australian embassy or consulate for an emergency travel document. If you are arrested, seriously ill or caught in a crisis, consular staff can help, and the 24 hour Consular Emergency Centre can be reached from anywhere. Smartraveller lists the numbers. But the Consular Services Charter is clear about limits. The government cannot pay your medical bills or legal fees, get you out of jail or override local laws. That is exactly why insurance and good preparation matter. Before you leave, make sure someone at home has your itinerary and knows how to reach you, and check in with them regularly."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why check your passport expiry well before travel?", ["Passports expire on your birthday", "Many countries require validity for months beyond your trip", "Airlines charge more for older passports", "It is needed for travel insurance"], 1, "Many countries want at least six months' validity remaining."],
    [2, "What is the highest Smartraveller advice level?", ["Reconsider your need to travel", "Exercise a high degree of caution", "Do not travel", "Exercise normal safety precautions"], 2, "'Do not travel' is the most serious of the four advice levels."],
    [3, "When is the best time to buy travel insurance?", ["At the airport", "After you arrive", "Only if you get sick", "When you book the trip"], 3, "Buying early means cancellation cover protects you before departure."],
    [5, "What do reciprocal health care agreements do?", ["Replace travel insurance entirely", "Cover some medically necessary care in certain countries", "Pay for evacuation flights", "Waive visa requirements"], 1, "They provide limited cover in some countries and do not replace insurance."],
    [6, "Which of these can the Australian government NOT do for you overseas?", ["Issue an emergency travel document", "Pay your medical bills", "Provide consular assistance", "Offer a 24 hour emergency line"], 1, "The Consular Services Charter says the government cannot pay medical or legal bills."]
  ]);

WB.L("making-a-will", "life", "intermediate", "Making a will, and why it matters", "Nobody likes thinking about it, but a will is one of the kindest things you can leave the people you love. Here is what it does and how to get one right.",
  [
    ["What happens without one", "If you die without a valid will, the law decides who gets what, using a fixed formula.", "Imagine a couple who have lived together for years but never married, with a child from an earlier relationship. One dies suddenly without a will. Their partner may face a slow and stressful process to prove the relationship, while assets are divided according to a formula set out in state or territory law, not by what the person would have wanted. Dying without a will is called dying intestate. The rules generally favour spouses, partners, children and close relatives in a set order, and they do not cover friends, charities or stepchildren in the way many people expect. A clear will spares your family guesswork, delay and, often, conflict at the worst possible time."],
    ["What a will does", "A will names who receives your property, who manages your estate and who should care for young children.", "At its simplest, a will is a written document setting out what happens to your estate, meaning the property you own when you die. It names beneficiaries, the people or organisations who inherit, and says what each receives. It names an executor, the person responsible for gathering your assets, paying debts and distributing what is left. If you have children under 18, it can also name a guardian you would like to care for them. A will can include specific gifts, such as a ring to a niece, as well as the 'residue', which is everything else. It can also set out wishes about funerals, though these are not always legally binding."],
    ["Choosing an executor", "Pick someone trustworthy and organised, and ask them first, because the role is real work.", "Being an executor is not an honour you frame on the wall. It can mean months of paperwork: locating documents, dealing with banks, possibly applying to the Supreme Court for a grant of probate, selling property and paying tax obligations. Choose someone you trust, who is organised and likely to outlive you, and ask them before naming them. Many people pick a partner or adult child, often with a backup in case the first cannot act. Some appoint a professional, such as a solicitor or the state trustee organisation, which charges fees but brings experience and neutrality. Naming two people who do not get along as joint executors can cause exactly the conflict you hoped to avoid."],
    ["Making it valid", "A will must generally be in writing, signed by you and witnessed by two adults present together.", "The formal rules vary a little between states and territories, but the core is similar. The will must be in writing, signed by the person making it, and signed by two adult witnesses who are both present when you sign. A witness who is also a beneficiary, or a beneficiary's partner, can create problems, and in some places the gift to them may be at risk, so use independent witnesses. Courts can sometimes accept informal documents, even unsigned notes or videos, but proving them is slow and expensive. Do-it-yourself will kits exist and can work for very simple situations, yet small mistakes are common. This is general information, not legal advice."],
    ["Things a will does not control", "Superannuation and jointly owned assets often pass outside your will, so plan them separately.", "Many Australians are surprised to learn that their superannuation does not automatically follow their will. Super is held in trust, and the fund trustee decides who receives it unless you have made a valid binding death benefit nomination. Some binding nominations lapse after a few years unless renewed, so check with your fund. Life insurance held inside super follows the same path. Property owned as joint tenants, such as a home held jointly by a couple, usually passes straight to the surviving owner regardless of the will. Bank accounts held jointly can work the same way. Reviewing these alongside your will is essential, or your careful plan may only cover a fraction of what you own."],
    ["Getting help and keeping it current", "Solicitors and state public trustees can prepare wills, and life changes mean it is time to review.", "For most people, a will prepared by a solicitor is money well spent, particularly with blended families, a business, overseas assets or a family member who may contest. Each state and territory also has a public trustee or state trustee organisation that can prepare wills, sometimes at low cost. Once it is signed, store the original somewhere safe and tell your executor where. Then review it whenever life changes. In most states, getting married revokes an existing will unless it was made in contemplation of that marriage, and divorce can cancel gifts to a former spouse. Having children, separating, buying property or a beneficiary dying are all good reasons to check that the will still says what you mean."],
    ["Beyond the will", "An enduring power of attorney and an advance care directive cover decisions while you are alive.", "A will only speaks after death. If an accident or illness left you unable to make decisions, a will would not help. That is the job of two other documents. An enduring power of attorney lets someone you trust manage your financial and legal affairs if you cannot. An enduring guardianship or similar appointment, along with an advance care directive, covers health and lifestyle decisions and records your wishes about medical treatment. The names and forms differ between states, so look up the official forms for where you live, or ask a solicitor. Talking openly with your family about your wishes is uncomfortable for a few minutes and an enormous gift for years."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is it called when someone dies without a valid will?", ["Probate", "Intestacy", "Executorship", "Revocation"], 1, "Dying without a valid will is called dying intestate, and a legal formula applies."],
    [2, "What does an executor do?", ["Witnesses the will", "Inherits everything", "Manages and distributes the estate", "Cares for the children"], 2, "The executor gathers assets, pays debts and distributes what remains."],
    [3, "Why should will witnesses usually not be beneficiaries?", ["It can put their gift at risk", "Beneficiaries cannot read", "Witnesses must be lawyers", "It makes the will too long"], 0, "A beneficiary witness can cause problems and may jeopardise their gift."],
    [4, "What controls who receives your superannuation when you die?", ["Your will, automatically", "The fund trustee, guided by any valid binding nomination", "Your bank", "Your executor alone"], 1, "Super is held in trust and does not automatically follow your will."],
    [5, "In most Australian states, what happens to an existing will when you marry?", ["It is usually revoked unless made in contemplation of the marriage", "It becomes stronger", "Nothing at all", "It automatically names your spouse executor"], 0, "Marriage generally revokes a will unless it was made with that marriage in mind."]
  ]);

WB.L("complaints-that-get-results", "life", "advanced", "Making a complaint that actually gets results", "Knowing your rights is one thing; getting a business to honour them is another. Here is the step by step path from polite email to independent ombudsman.",
  [
    ["Start with a clear goal", "Before you complain, decide exactly what outcome you want and what the law says you are entitled to.", "Consider a washing machine that dies a fortnight after its warranty ends. Angry phone calls get nowhere. What works is clarity. Before you contact the business, write down what went wrong, when, and what you want: a repair, a replacement, a refund or compensation. Then check what Australian Consumer Law says. Its consumer guarantees mean goods must be of acceptable quality and last a reasonable time, regardless of any manufacturer's warranty, and a major failure gives you a choice of remedy. The ACCC's website, accc.gov.au, explains the guarantees in plain English. Knowing your rights lets you ask calmly for something specific, which is far harder to dismiss than general frustration. This is general information, not legal advice."],
    ["Gather your evidence", "Receipts, photos, dates and names turn a story into a case.", "Complaints succeed on evidence. Collect proof of purchase, such as a receipt, bank statement or order confirmation. Photograph or video the fault, ideally showing it happening. Keep the original packaging and any warranty documents if you still have them. Most importantly, start a simple log: every call, email and visit, with the date, the person you dealt with and what they said or promised. If a salesperson made a claim about what the product could do, note it, because misleading representations are a separate problem under the law. This record does two jobs. It makes your complaint precise, and if you later need an ombudsman or tribunal, it shows that you tried reasonably to resolve things first."],
    ["Complain to the business in writing", "Contact the business first, then follow up in writing so there is a clear record.", "Most problems are fixed at the first step, so give the business a genuine chance. Ask to speak with someone who can make decisions, often a manager or the complaints team rather than front counter staff. Be polite, specific and firm. If that does not work, put your complaint in writing by email or letter. Briefly set out what you bought, the problem, what you have done so far and the remedy you want. Refer to the consumer guarantees if relevant, and give a reasonable deadline, such as 14 days, for a response. Many large businesses must have an internal dispute resolution process, and asking for your complaint to be formally logged often moves it to more senior people."],
    ["Escalate to the right body", "If the business will not budge, go to your state fair trading agency or the relevant ombudsman.", "Where you go next depends on the industry. For most goods and services, contact the fair trading or consumer affairs agency in your state or territory, such as NSW Fair Trading or Consumer Affairs Victoria. They can give advice and may contact the business on your behalf. The ACCC enforces the law across Australia but generally does not resolve individual disputes, though reporting to it helps reveal patterns. Several industries have their own free, independent ombudsman schemes. The Telecommunications Industry Ombudsman handles phone and internet complaints, the Australian Financial Complaints Authority covers banks, insurers and super funds, and each state has an energy and water ombudsman. Most require you to complain to the business first."],
    ["How ombudsman schemes work", "Ombudsmen are free for consumers and can make binding decisions on member businesses.", "An ombudsman scheme is a free, independent service that tries to resolve disputes between consumers and businesses in a particular industry. You lodge a complaint, usually online, and the scheme refers it back to the business's senior staff for a final chance to fix it. Many complaints settle at this point, because businesses often pay fees when complaints progress. If not, the scheme investigates and may recommend or decide an outcome. In schemes like AFCA, a decision you accept can bind the business, while you remain free to go to court instead if you reject it. Time limits apply, so lodge promptly. The process is designed for ordinary people, and you do not need a lawyer."],
    ["Tribunals and chargebacks", "Small claims tribunals and card chargebacks are powerful tools when other steps fail.", "If no ombudsman covers the business and the fair trading agency cannot resolve it, a state tribunal or local court can hear consumer claims. Examples include NCAT in New South Wales, VCAT in Victoria and QCAT in Queensland. Small claims processes are designed to be cheaper and less formal than ordinary courts, though there is usually a filing fee and you present your own case with your evidence. Separately, if you paid by credit or debit card and the goods never arrived or the business has collapsed, ask your bank about a chargeback. Card schemes have rules for disputed transactions, and banks set time limits for requests, so contact them as soon as you realise there is a problem."],
    ["Tone, persistence and fairness", "Stay calm, factual and persistent, and be open to a fair compromise.", "The most effective complainers are rarely the loudest. Staff who are shouted at tend to follow the script; staff who are treated respectfully often look for a way to help. Keep your messages short and factual, repeat your requested remedy clearly, and do not accept vague promises without a date. If you are offered something less than you asked for, consider whether it is fair rather than rejecting it on principle, since time and stress have costs too. Public reviews and social media posts can attract attention, but stick to accurate facts to avoid defamation risks. Finally, report scams and misleading conduct to the ACCC or Scamwatch, even if you get your money back. It helps protect the next customer."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Under the consumer guarantees, when can you get a remedy for a faulty product?", ["Only during the manufacturer's warranty", "Only with the original box", "When goods fail to be of acceptable quality, even after the warranty ends", "Only for items over $1000"], 2, "Consumer guarantees apply regardless of any manufacturer's warranty period."],
    [1, "Why keep a log of calls and emails?", ["To make the complaint precise and show you tried to resolve it", "Because businesses require it", "To post on social media", "It is a legal requirement"], 0, "A log strengthens your case at every later step."],
    [3, "Which body handles phone and internet complaints?", ["AFCA", "The ACCC", "The Commonwealth Ombudsman", "The Telecommunications Industry Ombudsman"], 3, "The TIO is the free ombudsman for telco disputes."],
    [4, "What do most ombudsman schemes require before they will take your complaint?", ["A lawyer's letter", "That you first complained to the business", "A filing fee", "A court order"], 1, "You usually need to give the business a chance to fix it first."],
    [5, "If you paid by card and the goods never arrived, what can you ask your bank for?", ["A chargeback", "A loan", "A refund from the ACCC", "A new card number only"], 0, "Card schemes have chargeback rules for disputed transactions, with time limits."]
  ]);

WB.L("building-a-better-sleep-routine", "life", "beginner", "Building a better sleep routine", "Good sleep is less about a magic pill and more about a handful of habits done consistently. Here is a practical routine you can start tonight.",
  [
    ["Anchor your wake time", "Getting up at the same time every day is the single strongest lever for steadier sleep.", "Many people try to fix bad sleep by going to bed earlier, then lie awake staring at the ceiling. A more reliable starting point is the other end of the night. Your body clock, the circadian rhythm, runs on a roughly 24 hour cycle and is set largely by when you wake and see light. Choose a wake time you can keep seven days a week, within about half an hour, including weekends. Big Saturday sleep-ins shift your clock later, which is part of why Monday mornings feel brutal. After a week or two of a steady wake time, many people find they naturally feel sleepy at a more regular hour. This is general information, not medical advice."],
    ["Use light as your clock", "Bright light in the morning and dim light at night help your body know what time it is.", "Light is the main signal your brain uses to set its clock. Getting outside within an hour or so of waking, even on a cloudy day, gives a much stronger signal than indoor lighting. A short walk, breakfast on the balcony or a coffee by an open window all count. In the evening, the opposite helps. Dimming the house lights an hour or two before bed, using lamps instead of bright overheads, and lowering screen brightness all send the message that night is coming. Many phones and computers have a night mode that warms the colour of the screen. In Australian summers, block-out curtains can stop early sunrise from waking you before you are ready."],
    ["Watch caffeine and alcohol", "Caffeine lingers for hours and alcohol fragments sleep, so time them carefully.", "That 3 pm flat white may feel harmless, but caffeine stays in your system for several hours, and for some people much longer. It blocks adenosine, a chemical that builds up while you are awake and makes you feel sleepy. A common suggestion is to have your last caffeine around midday or early afternoon, and to remember hidden sources such as energy drinks, cola, some teas and dark chocolate. Alcohol is trickier because it can make you drowsy at first. Later in the night, though, it tends to disrupt sleep, causing lighter, more broken rest and more trips to the bathroom. Heavy meals close to bedtime can also cause discomfort, so try to eat dinner a few hours before bed."],
    ["Create a wind-down ritual", "A predictable 30 to 60 minute routine before bed tells your brain it is time to switch off.", "Children have bedtime routines for good reason: bath, book, lights out. Adults benefit from the same idea. Pick a few calm activities and do them in roughly the same order each night for the last half hour to an hour before bed. That might be tidying the kitchen, a warm shower, setting out tomorrow's clothes, reading a paper book and some slow breathing. Keep work emails and gripping news out of this window, since they can switch your brain back into problem solving mode. If worries race through your head, try writing a quick to-do list for tomorrow before you start winding down. Getting tasks onto paper can make them easier to let go of."],
    ["Set up the bedroom", "A cool, dark, quiet and comfortable room makes sleep easier to start and keep.", "Your bedroom works best when it is mainly for sleep. Most people sleep better in a slightly cool room, so in hot Australian summers a fan, air conditioning set to a comfortable level or lighter bedding can make a real difference. Darkness helps too: block-out curtains or an eye mask can cut light from street lamps and early dawn. For noise, earplugs or a steady background sound such as a fan can mask barking dogs and traffic. Check that your mattress and pillow still support you, since both wear out. Finally, consider charging your phone outside the bedroom and using a cheap alarm clock instead, which removes the temptation to scroll at midnight."],
    ["If you cannot sleep", "If you are awake for a long while, get up and do something calm until you feel sleepy.", "Lying in bed for an hour willing yourself to sleep can teach your brain to link bed with frustration. Sleep specialists often suggest a different approach. If you have been awake for what feels like twenty minutes or so, without clock watching, get up and go to another dim room. Do something quiet and boring, such as reading or folding washing, then return to bed only when you feel sleepy. Repeat if needed. It feels counterintuitive, but over time it rebuilds the connection between bed and sleep. Keep naps short, ideally under half an hour and before mid afternoon, so they do not steal sleep from the night. Avoid looking at the time repeatedly, which tends to increase anxiety."],
    ["When to get help", "Ongoing poor sleep, loud snoring or gasping, or daytime sleepiness are reasons to see a GP.", "Most adults are thought to need roughly seven to nine hours of sleep, though individuals vary. Occasional bad nights are normal, but persistent trouble is worth taking seriously. See your GP if poor sleep lasts for weeks and affects your daytime life, if a partner notices loud snoring, pauses in breathing or gasping, if you feel very sleepy during the day, especially while driving, or if you have strong urges to move your legs at night. These can point to treatable sleep conditions. Effective help exists, including a structured talking therapy for insomnia known as CBT-I. The Sleep Health Foundation and healthdirect have reliable information, and this lesson is general information, not medical advice."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is suggested as the strongest lever for steadier sleep?", ["Going to bed much earlier", "Keeping a consistent wake time every day", "Long weekend sleep-ins", "Napping in the afternoon"], 1, "A steady wake time anchors your body clock."],
    [1, "Why get outside soon after waking?", ["Morning light strongly sets your body clock", "To burn calories", "It cools the body", "It replaces caffeine entirely"], 0, "Bright morning light is the main signal that sets the circadian rhythm."],
    [2, "What does alcohol tend to do to sleep later in the night?", ["Deepen it", "Make it more refreshing", "Disrupt it, making it lighter and more broken", "Have no effect"], 2, "Alcohol may cause drowsiness at first but fragments sleep later."],
    [5, "If you have been awake in bed for a long while, what is often suggested?", ["Check the clock regularly", "Scroll your phone in bed", "Have a coffee", "Get up, do something calm in dim light, and return when sleepy"], 3, "This helps rebuild the link between bed and sleep."],
    [6, "Which of these is a reason to see a GP about sleep?", ["One bad night before an exam", "Snoring with pauses in breathing or gasping", "Waking once to use the bathroom", "Dreaming vividly occasionally"], 1, "Pauses in breathing or gasping can signal a treatable sleep condition."]
  ]);

WB.L("private-health-insurance-decoded", "life", "advanced", "Private health insurance, decoded", "Gold, Silver, Bronze, extras, excesses and a mysterious loading after 30. Here is how private health insurance really works in Australia, so you can decide what you need.",
  [
    ["How it fits with Medicare", "Private cover sits on top of Medicare, mainly for choice of doctor, timing and private hospital care.", "Every Australian resident enrolled in Medicare can be treated free as a public patient in a public hospital. So why do roughly half of Australians hold private hospital cover? Mostly for choice and timing. As a private patient you can usually choose your specialist and hospital, and elective surgery such as a knee reconstruction may happen sooner than on a public waiting list. Private cover can also help with services Medicare does not cover, such as dental, physiotherapy and glasses, through extras policies. There are tax rules that push some people towards cover as well. Whether it is worth it depends on your health, age, income and priorities. This is general information, not financial advice."],
    ["Hospital cover and its tiers", "Hospital policies are sorted into Gold, Silver, Bronze and Basic tiers based on what treatments they must cover.", "Hospital cover pays towards your costs as a private patient in hospital, such as accommodation, theatre fees and part of the doctors' fees. Since reforms in 2019, policies are grouped into four tiers: Gold, Silver, Bronze and Basic. Each tier must cover a minimum list of clinical categories. Gold covers every category, including pregnancy and birth, joint replacements and assisted reproductive services. Lower tiers cover fewer categories, so a cheap Basic policy may cover very little. Some policies are labelled 'plus', meaning they cover extra categories above their tier's minimum. When comparing policies, look at the specific treatments you might realistically need in the next few years, rather than just the tier name or the price."],
    ["Extras cover", "Extras policies pay benefits for things like dental and physio, but limits and gaps mean you rarely get full cost back.", "Extras, sometimes called general treatment, pays part of the cost of services outside hospital such as dental check-ups, physiotherapy, optical, chiropractic and remedial massage. It is sold separately or combined with hospital cover. The catch is that extras rarely pays the full bill. Each service has a benefit amount and an annual limit, and you pay the rest. A quick test is to add up what you actually spend on these services in a typical year, then compare it with the premium plus the gaps you would still pay. For some people, especially those with regular dental or physio needs, it comes out ahead. For others, putting the premium into a savings account works better."],
    ["Excesses, gaps and waiting periods", "Know what you pay at the time of treatment and how long before you can claim.", "Two policies with the same tier can cost very different amounts because of the excess, the amount you agree to pay upfront if you are admitted to hospital. A higher excess lowers your premium but raises your bill when you need care. You may also face a gap, the difference between what doctors charge and what Medicare and your insurer pay. Ask your specialist and hospital about likely gaps before treatment, and ask whether they have an arrangement with your fund. Waiting periods apply when you first take out cover or upgrade. They are commonly up to 12 months for pre-existing conditions and pregnancy, and shorter for most other hospital treatment. Check the specifics before you need them."],
    ["Lifetime Health Cover loading", "If you take out hospital cover later in life, you may pay an extra loading on your premium for years.", "Lifetime Health Cover is designed to encourage people to take out hospital cover earlier. If you do not hold private hospital cover by 1 July following your 31st birthday, a loading of 2 per cent is added to your hospital premium for every year you are over 30 when you eventually take it out, up to a maximum of 70 per cent. So someone first joining at 40 would typically pay around 20 per cent more than someone who joined at 30. The loading is removed after you have held hospital cover continuously for 10 years. There are some exceptions, including for people who arrived in Australia later in life. Extras-only cover does not count towards avoiding the loading."],
    ["The Medicare Levy Surcharge and rebate", "Higher earners without hospital cover may pay an extra tax, and the government rebate on premiums is income tested.", "The Medicare Levy Surcharge is an extra tax charged to people whose income is above a threshold and who do not hold an appropriate level of private hospital cover. It is separate from the ordinary Medicare levy most taxpayers pay. For some people on higher incomes, the surcharge would cost more than a basic hospital policy, which is why some buy cover mainly for tax reasons. Separately, the Australian Government Rebate reduces premiums for many people, with the amount depending on income and age. Thresholds and percentages change regularly, so check the current figures on the ATO website, ato.gov.au, rather than relying on old numbers. A tax agent can help you apply the rules to your own situation."],
    ["Comparing and switching", "Use the government's comparison site, compare like with like, and review your cover regularly.", "Insurers have thousands of policies, and the names alone tell you very little. The government run website privatehealth.gov.au, managed by the Commonwealth Ombudsman, lets you compare policies from all registered funds, and every policy has a standard information statement summarising what it covers. Commercial comparison sites can be useful, but they may not include every fund. You can switch funds, and if you move to an equivalent level of hospital cover you generally will not serve the same waiting periods again, though upgrades may attract new ones. Review your cover each year, especially when life changes, such as planning a family or retiring. If you have a dispute with your fund, the Private Health Insurance Ombudsman can help, free of charge."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is a main reason people hold private hospital cover?", ["To access Medicare", "Choice of doctor and timing for elective care", "To avoid all costs in public hospitals", "It is compulsory"], 1, "Private cover mainly offers choice of specialist and hospital, and often shorter waits."],
    [1, "Which hospital tier must cover every clinical category?", ["Basic", "Bronze", "Silver", "Gold"], 3, "Gold policies cover all clinical categories, including pregnancy and joint replacements."],
    [3, "What is the effect of choosing a higher excess?", ["Lower premiums but a bigger bill when admitted", "Higher premiums and no bill", "No waiting periods", "Full cover for extras"], 0, "A higher excess trades a lower premium for more upfront cost when you need care."],
    [4, "Under Lifetime Health Cover, what loading applies for each year you are over 30 when you first take hospital cover?", ["1 per cent", "2 per cent", "5 per cent", "10 per cent"], 1, "The loading is 2 per cent a year, capped at 70 per cent, and removed after 10 years."],
    [6, "Which website lets you compare policies from all registered funds?", ["privatehealth.gov.au", "smartraveller.gov.au", "scamwatch.gov.au", "ppsr.gov.au"], 0, "privatehealth.gov.au, run by the Commonwealth Ombudsman, covers all registered funds."]
  ]);
